# Trivy config-scan exemptions.
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS FILE
#
#   1. An entry must name a control Trivy actually has, and the reason must be
#      about THAT control. An invented mapping ("AVD-KSV-0121 covers our NFS
#      export not using Kerberos") reads like a considered decision and silences
#      a real finding. The KSV family is pod-security and RBAC; it has no
#      storage-protocol checks at all.
#   2. An entry must be capable of firing. `.github/workflows/ci.yaml` gates on
#      `severity: HIGH,CRITICAL`, so a LOW/MEDIUM rule is already out of scope
#      and listing it here is a no-op. Likewise a rule that can only fire on
#      manifests this repo does not contain.
#   3. An entry needs a reason and, ideally, a pointer to the runbook that would
#      remove it.
#
# Verify what Trivy actually reports before adding anything:
#
#     trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignorefile /dev/null .
#
# Format: one check ID per line.

# ###########################################################################
# # TRIAGE, 2026-09-01. 39 findings: 2 CRITICAL, 37 HIGH.
# #
# # Reproduce exactly what produced this list:
# #
# #     trivy config . --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignorefile /dev/null \
# #       --skip-check-update
# #
# # `--skip-check-update` matters on a slow link: without it Trivy tries to pull
# # the checks bundle from a registry, times out after several minutes and then
# # dies rather than falling back. With it, the embedded checks are used. CI does
# # download the bundle, so its count can differ from a local run by one or two --
# # do not treat the exact number as a fixed fact.
# #
# # Policy applied here: anything CRITICAL is resolved consciously. Anything HIGH
# # whose "fix" would plausibly break a working system is deferred and named,
# # not silenced.
# ###########################################################################

# --- CRITICAL: the barman-cloud plugin ClusterRole --------------------------
# AVD-KSV-0041  Manage secrets
# AVD-KSV-0050  Manage Kubernetes RBAC resources
#
# Both are `ClusterRole/plugin-barman-cloud`, and both come from VENDORED
# UPSTREAM. platform/barman-cloud-plugin/kustomization.yaml has exactly one
# resource:
#
#     https://github.com/cloudnative-pg/plugin-barman-cloud/releases/download/
#       v0.12.0/manifest.yaml
#
# so this RBAC is the plugin author's, not this repository's. Editing it means
# patching an upstream release bundle, and the thing it would break is Postgres
# backups -- including Immich's database, which is the data this whole repository
# exists to protect.
#
# THE ACTUAL PERMISSIONS ARE NARROWER THAN THE FINDINGS SUGGEST. Read from
# deploy/platform/barman-cloud-plugin/manifests.yaml:
#
#     secrets            create, delete, get, list, watch   (no update/patch, no *)
#     roles/rolebindings create, get, list                  (no update/delete, no *)
#
# KSV-0050's message enumerates the verb set it TESTS for
# (create/update/delete/deletecollection/impersonate/*); the role holds only
# `create`. The finding fires on that alone.
#
# Why the plugin needs them: it creates the per-Cluster credential and TLS
# Secrets for its CNPG-I connection, and creates per-Cluster Role/RoleBinding so
# each Postgres instance can read its own ObjectStore configuration.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK, STATED PLAINLY: a compromise of the plugin pod in `cnpg-system`
# can read and create Secrets cluster-wide and create RBAC. That is a real
# escalation path and it is accepted, not dismissed. What bounds it: the version
# is pinned, Renovate groups it with the cloudnative-pg chart so both are reviewed
# in one PR, and the bump is the moment to re-read these rules.
#
# REVISIT WHEN: the plugin offers a namespace-scoped install, or the bundle is
# ever forked locally for another reason.
AVD-KSV-0041
AVD-KSV-0050

# --- FALSE POSITIVE, proven -------------------------------------------------
# AVD-KSV-0109  ConfigMap with secrets
#
# 4 hits, all on backup-verify's script ConfigMaps, and the "secret" Trivy
# reports is the literal string:
#
#     echo "PASS
#
# from `echo "PASS: $TAG"` in platform/backup-verify/restore-files.yaml:263 and
# `echo "PASS: $SRC restored and verified"` in restore-postgres.yaml:276. The
# check matches `PASS` as the start of `PASSWORD`.
#
# There is no credential in either ConfigMap; the restore drills read their
# credentials from the `s3-backup` Secret via envFrom. Verified by grep, not
# assumed -- and the two source hits plus their two rendered copies in deploy/
# account for all four.
#
# Note what this does NOT excuse: a real secret pasted into one of these
# ConfigMaps would now go unreported by THIS check. scripts/leak-check.sh is the
# control that still covers it, and it scans deploy/ as well as the sources.
AVD-KSV-0109

# --- DEFERRED: pods/exec in the restore drill -------------------------------
# AVD-KSV-0053  Exec into Pods
#
# `Role/backup-verify` (namespaced, NOT a ClusterRole) can create `pods/exec` in
# the `backup-verify` namespace.
#
# That is how the drill works: platform/backup-verify/restore-postgres.yaml
# restores a dump into a throwaway CNPG cluster and then execs `psql` into it to
# compare table counts, row counts and extension sets against the source.
# Removing the verb removes the only mechanism in this repository that proves a
# backup can actually be restored.
#
# Scope that makes it acceptable: it is a Role, so it cannot exec into pods in any
# other namespace, and `backup-verify` contains only the verification jobs and the
# ephemeral restore targets they create. There is no long-lived workload there to
# pivot into.
#
# REVISIT WHEN: the drill can verify a restore without exec -- e.g. by connecting
# to the restored cluster over its Service with a client in the job's own pod.
# That is a rewrite of restore-postgres.yaml, not a permission change, and it is
# worth doing eventually.
AVD-KSV-0053

# --- DEFERRED: S3 bucket encryption mode ------------------------------------
# AVD-AWS-0132  S3 encryption should use Customer Managed Keys
#
# Already a documented decision, not an oversight:
# bootstrap/aws-backup/main.tf:103 chooses SSE-S3 (AES256) with
# `bucket_key_enabled`, because restic encrypts client-side with RESTIC_PASSWORD
# before anything is uploaded, and SSE-KMS bills per request -- which on WAL
# archiving means per segment, forever.
#
# THE PART THAT COMMENT DOES NOT SAY, and which is the real content of this
# finding: the barman/Postgres objects are NOT client-side encrypted. For those,
# SSE-S3 is the only encryption at rest, and its key is AWS-held. So the gap this
# rule points at is narrower than "the bucket is unencrypted" but it is not
# nothing.
#
# Two ways to close it, when the bucket actually exists (`tofu apply` has never
# been run -- every destinationPath still says homelab-backups-REPLACE-ME):
#   - SSE-KMS with a CMK, accepting the per-segment charge and a new key whose
#     loss destroys the Postgres backups; or
#   - barman's own `encryption` setting, giving the Postgres objects the same
#     client-side property restic already has, at no AWS cost.
#
# The second is probably the right answer and is a change to the CNPG Cluster
# specs, not to Terraform. Deferred because the bucket does not exist and because
# introducing a second unrecoverable key deserves a deliberate decision.
AVD-AWS-0132

# --- DEFERRED: no explicit securityContext on the media workloads -----------
# AVD-KSV-0118  Default security context configured
#
# 30 of the 37 HIGH findings, and the reason it is deferred rather than fixed is
# that the fix breaks the images.
#
# Every workload it fires on is a linuxserver/hotio-style container -- the *arr
# apps, Plex, Jellyfin, qBittorrent, Seafile, Paperless, Mealie, Syncthing. They
# start an s6 supervision tree as ROOT, chown their config and media paths, then
# drop to PUID/PGID 1000. Setting `runAsNonRoot: true` or a non-root `runAsUser`
# makes that entrypoint fail before the application starts.
#
# What they do have: pod-level `fsGroup: 1000` with
# `fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch`, and a namespace-wide PUID/PGID/UMASK
# contract that apps/theater/sonarr.yaml documents -- changing it for one app
# breaks hardlinked imports for all of them.
#
# This is the same position `.kube-linter.yaml` takes on the equivalent check and
# the same reasoning as AVD-KSV-0014 below. One control, one answer, in all three
# places.
#
# NOT deferred for the workloads that CAN satisfy it, and this is the line worth
# holding: every container this repository authors itself -- the restic backup
# jobs, the restore drills, oauth2-proxy, Recyclarr -- runs
# `runAsNonRoot: true`, `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` and `drop: ["ALL"]`. The
# exemption is for images whose entrypoint requires root, not for new work.
#
# REVISIT WHEN: an upstream image supports a rootless entrypoint, or is replaced.
# The per-app fix is local, so this can be retired incrementally rather than all
# at once.
AVD-KSV-0118

# --- read-only root filesystem ----------------------------------------------
# 18 of 22 workload containers do not set readOnlyRootFilesystem. Several
# upstream images (Keycloak, the *arr apps, Plex, Seafile, Paperless, Mealie)
# write to their own filesystem at runtime and provide no way to relocate that,
# so enforcing a read-only root would mean forking every image.
#
# `.kube-linter.yaml` holds the SAME position for the equivalent kube-linter
# check (`no-read-only-root-fs`), which is listed there as advisory rather than
# blocking. The two files must not disagree: one control, one answer.
AVD-KSV-0014

# --- NOT exempted, and why ---------------------------------------------------
# hostPath / hostPID / hostNetwork / privileged (the AVD-KSV-0009 and
# AVD-KSV-0023 family): the workloads that genuinely need these -- the
# democratic-csi node plugin driving the host iSCSI initiator, and
# node-exporter -- come from upstream Helm charts. `scripts/render-deploy.sh`
# deliberately does not render charts, so those Pod specs never appear in this
# repo and Trivy's config scan cannot see them. Exempting them would be
# exempting nothing.
#
# The actual control is the namespace label
# `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: privileged` in
# infrastructure/namespaces/namespaces.yaml. If a chart is ever vendored into
# deploy/, these IDs come back here with that as the reason.
